course: dictionary
lesson: 12587
title: Formal words: disadvantageous
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Disadvantageous is a formal word, but you can work it out from its parts. An advantage is something that helps you, and dis- means not or the opposite. So disadvantageous means not helpful, putting someone in a worse position. «Losing both goalkeepers before the match was disadvantageous for the team.» The clue is losing: the team now has a harder time winning.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping in an easier word. «Losing both goalkeepers before the match was bad for the team» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. Disadvantageous just sounds more formal, the kind of word you find in exams, news and history books.
explain: Look for the words that often come with it: disadvantageous to, disadvantageous for, and a disadvantageous position. «The new rule was disadvantageous to small shops.» The word to or for tells you who is put in the worse position.
fact: Disadvantageous means causing a disadvantage; not helpful.
fact: A disadvantage is something that puts you in a worse position than other people.
fact: «A disadvantageous deal» is a deal that is unfair or harmful to one side.
fact: Disadvantageous is a formal word, mostly found in exams, maths word problems, history and the news.
fact: You will often see it with to or for: «disadvantageous to» and «disadvantageous for».
exercise: «Sharing one pencil between the whole class was disadvantageous for the children at the back.» What does disadvantageous mean here? | Helpful for everyone | *Not helpful, putting them in a worse position | Covered in ink | Very quiet
exercise: Anna says disadvantageous means lucky. Is she right? | Yes, it means lucky | *No, it means not helpful, putting someone in a worse position | Yes, it means cheerful | No, it means very loud
exercise: Choose the word that fits: «The new rule was ___ to the small shops.» | sleepy | sunny | *disadvantageous | cheerful
test: «The storm was disadvantageous to the fishing boats, which could not sail out.» What does disadvantageous mean? | Helpful for the boats | Quick and cheap | *Harmful, putting the boats in a worse position | Very colourful
test: Which phrase best matches «a disadvantageous position»? | *a weak place to start from | a bright, sunny room | a quick way home | a lucky start
exam: Choose the best word: «The unfair contract was ___ for the workers, who lost their pay.» | cheerful | sleepy | helpful | *disadvantageous
